          "body": "Closes #7043\n\n## The bug\n\n`close()` resolved, and `onClose` hooks ran, while a request was still executing — whenever that request arrived on the **secondary** listener created by the default dual-stack `listen({ port })`.\n\nVerified on current `main` (v6.0.0-alpha.4), not only the reported 5.12.5:\n\n```\n### SECONDARY (127.0.0.1) — before:\nclose() resolved after 0ms, inFlight = 1, onCloseInFlight = 1\n### PRIMARY (::1) — before (correct):\nclose() resolved after 1000ms, inFlight = 0, onCloseInClose = 0\n```\n\nThis contradicts the documented lifecycle in `docs/Reference/Server.md` — at step 5, \"`onClose` hooks execute. All in-flight requests have completed and the server is no longer listening\" — and the Hooks reference, which calls `onClose` \"the safe place for plugins to release resources such as database connection pools.\"\n\n## Root cause\n\n`lib/server.js:190-208` — `closeSecondary` calls `secondaryServer.close(() => {})` and **discards the callback**, and is wired to the main server's `unref`/`close`/`error` events. Meanwhile `fastify.js:417` gated `done()` on `instance.server.close(cb)` alone, and `instance.server` is the *main* server. Secondary servers are tracked in `kServerBindings` (`lib/server.js:179`), but that array was only ever read by `addresses()`.\n\nOn this machine `dns.lookup('localhost', {all: true})` → `[::1, 127.0.0.1]`, so the **main** server is `::1` and `127.0.0.1` is the **secondary** — which is why an IPv4 client always hit this.\n\nOn the relation to #4098: the reporter is right that they are distinct. #4098 was Node's idle-connection accounting, fixed in Node 18.7.0. This is purely Fastify dropping a callback. That also explains why binding `{ host: '0.0.0.0' }` appears to work — no dual-stack, no secondary, nothing to drop.\n\n## The fix\n\n`fastify.js` only:\n\n1. `fastify.js:417-431` — the `done()` gate now also awaits `Promise.all(fastify[kServerBindings].map(closeBinding))`.\n2. `fastify.js:862-873` — a 9-line `closeBinding` helper that promisifies `server.close()`.\n\nIt is minimal because it only *adds* an await over data Fastify already tracks; it does not touch the `preClose` sequencing the existing comment warns about. Ordering is preserved: secondaries are still closed on the main server's `close` event, so nothing closes before `preClose` runs — we merely stop ignoring the result. The primary listener's path is byte-for-byte unchanged.\n\n`2 files changed, 118 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)`.\n\n## Tests\n\n`test/close.test.js`, two cases:\n\n- **control** — `close()` drains an in-flight request on the main binding. Passes before *and* after, proving no primary regression.\n- **regression** — same for a secondary binding. Fails before, passes after.\n\n**Determinism:** the request is pinned to a *specific* address via `http.get({ host: address.address, ... })`, so it provably lands on the secondary server rather than whichever family the client happens to pick. The handler blocks on a deferred that the test resolves later, so \"in flight\" is a fact rather than a guess — no sleeps anywhere. The barrier between `close()` and the release is a single `setImmediate`, which drains the microtask and `nextTick` queues; a `close()` that does not wait settles entirely within those and needs no I/O, so the buggy path has provably settled by then. 3/3 runs failed without the fix, 3/3 passed with it.\n\nThe shared helper skips cleanly when no secondary binding is created, so the test is meaningful on single-stack hosts too.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `unit` (borp): **1403 pass / 8 fail** — the *same* 8 failures as baseline, plus the 2 new passes.\n- `test:types`: 16 files, 1283 assertions, all pass.\n- `eslint` (full repo): clean. `lint:markdown` has 1 pre-existing error in `docs/Guides/Ecosystem.md` from `f7f73aa7`, not mine.\n- No `.only`, nothing skipped or weakened, no CI or dependency changes.\n\nThe 8 baseline failures are environmental, on `main` unmodified: three from `EADDRINUSE 127.0.0.1:3000` (another process held the port), and the rest from this box's `getaddrinfo` returning `::1` first for `localhost`, so `localhost` listeners bind `::1` while clients dial `127.0.0.1`. I confirmed they are unrelated by running the four affected files against `HEAD~1` — identical pass/fail counts with and without this change.\n\n## Two things for you to weigh\n\n1. **This is a real behaviour change on the 6.x line.** Anyone whose shutdown timing depended on `close()` resolving before secondary-listener requests finished is now correctly blocked. For a well-behaved app that is the fix; a handler hanging on a secondary socket will now hang shutdown. This matches Node's own `server.close()` semantics so I think it is correct, but it probably deserves a release note.\n2. **Unbounded waiting is worth a second opinion.** `kServerBindings` is only populated on successful secondary binds (`!_ignoreErr`), so failed bindings are correctly excluded. But a secondary whose close is *triggered and never completes* — a client holding a socket open — will now block `close()` forever, where before it was ignored. `forceCloseConnections: 'idle'` is the case most likely to expose this. The existing idle-mode tests pass, but I would rather you confirm that unbounded waiting is the intended trade than assume it.\n\nNo docs change needed: the code now matches what `docs/Reference/Server.md` and `docs/Reference/Hooks.md` already promise.\n\nFirst-time contributor, apologies if I've missed a convention.",
